 behind him. He has raised over $3.1 million and holds a massive cash-on-hand lead, while every major race rater places the contest in the Safe or Solid Republican column. Prediction markets give him roughly a 90 to 95 percent chance of winning.\n\nThe race took an unusual turn in August when the Democratic nominee withdrew and the party declined to replace her, leaving independent Brian Bengs as the sole challenger. Bengs has campaigned on denying either party a Senate majority, and recent polls show him within single digits, a notable improvement on earlier surveys. Still, the state's fundamentals, Rounds's money advantage, and the absence of a Democratic nominee make him the clear favorite, with Bengs needing an exceptional late break to threaten an upset.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.0278, "margin": -11.24}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.0347, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.0268, "margin": -12.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.0205, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.0193, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-27", "p_dem": 0.0201, "margin": -13.0}, {"date": "2026-09-28", "p_dem": 0.0185, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-29", "p_dem": 0.0147, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-09-30", "p_dem": 0.0242, "margin": -14.0}, {"date": "2026-10-01", "p_dem": 0.1443, "margin": -7.0}]}